WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.838
Onboarding runs in waves.

2
00:00:01.838 --> 00:00:06.825
Each wave creates its credential, connects
a defined set of servers, attaches the

3
00:00:06.825 --> 00:00:09.133
baseline on the same change, verifies,

4
00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:12.037
and closes by removing
what onboarding needed.

5
00:00:12.037 --> 00:00:16.866
Microsoft supports many methods: a
portal-generated script, PowerShell,

6
00:00:16.866 --> 00:00:20.809
a service principal with the
installation script, Ansible,

7
00:00:20.809 --> 00:00:24.914
Arc-enabled VMware vSphere, the
multicloud connector for AWS,

8
00:00:24.914 --> 00:00:29.099
Windows Admin Center, Configuration
Manager, and Group Policy.

9
00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:33.771
The program's recommendation is a dedicated
service principal that authenticates

10
00:00:33.771 --> 00:00:36.039
with a certificate, deployed through the

11
00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:38.575
automation tool the
server team already runs.

12
00:00:38.575 --> 00:00:42.537
The interactive script is for a
handful of servers or a pilot.

13
00:00:42.537 --> 00:00:45.959
The service principal exists
only to connect machines,

14
00:00:45.959 --> 00:00:49.902
and the credential is needed only
while azcmagent connect runs;

15
00:00:49.902 --> 00:00:53.100
if it expires afterward,
the server stays connected.

16
00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.938
So keep its lifetime
short and its scope small:

17
00:00:55.938 --> 00:00:59.000
the onboarding role, on
the target resource group.

18
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:01.826
Microsoft recommends a
certificate over a secret,

19
00:01:01.826 --> 00:01:06.200
because a certificate credential can
be controlled with Conditional Access.

20
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:10.873
If you must use a secret, Microsoft's
examples create one valid for a year;

21
00:01:10.873 --> 00:01:13.725
set it shorter and rotate
it after each wave.

22
00:01:13.725 --> 00:01:18.129
A stolen onboarding credential lets an
attacker connect servers outside your

23
00:01:18.129 --> 00:01:22.400
organization into your subscription,
and private endpoints close that path.

24
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.462
Protect the credential in deployment.

25
00:01:24.462 --> 00:01:27.060
A secret in a hand-built startup script is

26
00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.963
readable by every domain
user through SYSVOL.

27
00:01:29.963 --> 00:01:35.636
Microsoft's Group Policy method encrypts
its configuration file so only computer

28
00:01:35.636 --> 00:01:39.994
accounts can decrypt it, which
still means any computer account,

29
00:01:39.994 --> 00:01:45.338
so scope the GPO, rotate after the wave,
and disable the GPO once servers connect.

30
00:01:45.338 --> 00:01:49.645
For sensitive servers, set local
controls that Azure can't override:

31
00:01:49.645 --> 00:01:53.470
an extension allowlist, and
disabling remote connections.
